\section{Discussion and Conclusion}\label{sec:discussion}\label{sec:selection_method}\label{sec:conclusion}

\paragraph{Why test-time scaling fails for NER.}
For discrete-answer reasoning, sample diversity reflects diverse reasoning paths; for NER, degeneracy ($12$--$68$\%) eliminates candidate diversity while signal alignment collapse ($\alpha{\to}0$) prevents ranking.
We quantify how the well-known capability-diversity tradeoff \cite{yun2025price} manifests in NER, observing this across model families (5.5--74\%; Table~\ref{tab:frontier_sc_cross}) and scales (4B--72B; \S\ref{sec:scaling}).
Free-form decoding confirms this is model-intrinsic (within-instance LP std ratio $0.97{\times}$; \S\ref{sec:selection_gap}).
Our work differs from \citet{yao2026consistency} along three dimensions: (1)~\textbf{Granularity}---task-level plateau observation vs.\ instance-level failure decomposition; (2)~\textbf{Mechanism}---they document diminishing returns without diagnosing causes, while we identify degeneracy and LP compression as co-occurring failure conditions; (3)~\textbf{Actionability}---our per-instance diagnostic signals enable selective resource allocation (entity-level construction yields $+1.40$\,pp where conditions are favorable).

\paragraph{Practical implications.}
Measuring degeneracy and LP-F1 alignment at $N{=}8$ sufficed to predict scaling returns on our benchmarks ($\theta_{\text{crit}}{=}0.70$, stable across four NER datasets). Whether the decomposition generalizes beyond NER requires independent investigation.

\paragraph{When not to scale.}
Practitioners should measure instance degeneracy at $N{=}8$ before committing to repeated inference: if ${>}50$\% of instances produce identical outputs, scaling provides no selection benefit regardless of signal choice.
When degeneracy is below $20$\%, LP-F1 correlation should be assessed: $\rho{>}0.70$ suggests LP selection or entity-level construction may yield gains; below this threshold, greedy decoding is preferable.
Entity-level construction is most effective when both low degeneracy and LP-F1 alignment co-occur (Few-NERD: $D{=}13$\%, headroom${=}12.4$\,pp); weak alignment yields only marginal gains even at low degeneracy (SciERC: $+0.32$\,pp, $p{=}0.19$).

\paragraph{Open questions.}
Three directions remain: (1)~whether learned verifiers (e.g., reward models) can break the introspection boundary and exploit hidden-state quality signals that prompt-based methods cannot access; (2)~whether diversity-promoting decoding strategies can preserve candidate variety without sacrificing output quality, mitigating the capability-diversity tension; (3)~whether the diagnostic framework transfers to other structured prediction tasks (e.g., coreference resolution, syntactic parsing) where output spaces similarly resist equivalence-class aggregation.

\paragraph{Conclusion.}
The diagnostic framework---localizing failure to degeneracy and signal alignment collapse---is our primary contribution; extensions to RE and SQL are exploratory, and whether the capability-diversity tension generalizes beyond NER remains open.

\section{Threshold Regime Analysis}\label{sec:theory_scaling}

\subsection{Entity Construction as MBR Decoding}\label{sec:mbr_theory}

Entity-level construction selects $\hat{y}_{\textup{MBR}} = \{e \in E_\cup : \hat{p}(e) > \alpha/(\alpha{+}1)\}$ under balanced set-overlap utility; $\alpha{=}1$ recovers majority vote (Proposition~\ref{prop:construction_mbr}; Table~\ref{tab:entity_construction}; Appendix~\ref{app:mbr_proof}).
Signal-derived weights $w_i$ yield quality-weighted MBR: $\hat{p}_w(e) = \sum_{i} w_i \, \mathbf{1}[e {\in} y_i] / \sum_{i} w_i$; weighting improves when $w_i$ correlates with instance quality but degrades otherwise (LP on SciERC: $\rho{\approx}0.2$; \S\ref{sec:prescriptive}).
We select $\varepsilon{=}0.05$ at the elbow of the compression curve, where LP-best selection advantage transitions from $+1.5$\,pp (compressed) to $+4.6$\,pp (non-compressed; Appendix~\ref{app:fewnerd_epsilon}).

\subsection{Scaling and Inverse Scaling}\label{sec:scaling}

Oracle F1 follows $F_1(N) = a - b \cdot N^{-c}$ ($R^2 > 0.999$; Figure~\ref{fig:oracle_scaling}): Few-NERD ($c{\approx}0.61$) scales comparably to reasoning tasks \cite{brown2024monkeys}, SciERC converges more slowly ($c{\approx}0.35$), yet confidence-based aggregation recovers ${<}1$\,pp---a persistent gap.
The gap persists across scales and families: scaling from 8B to 14B FT increases oracle headroom ($+1.2$\,pp) with minimal greedy gain ($+0.9$\,pp), and GPT-5.5 (200 Few-NERD instances, zero-shot) exhibits the same pattern---50\% degeneracy, SJ $\rho{=}0.346$, MV $+1.4$\,pp yet 6.8\,pp oracle headroom unrealized.\footnote{Of the 10.1\% of GPT-5.5 samples producing empty entity sets, 88\% correspond to gold-empty instances; true parse failures affect only 1.2\% of samples (19/1{,}600) and shift all reported metrics by ${<}0.5$\,pp. See Appendix~\ref{app:frontier_sc}.}
Degeneracy is model-family-dependent rather than scale-dependent (Qwen2.5: $31.7$--$46.2$\% vs.\ Qwen3: $13.0$\% vs.\ GPT-5.5: $50$\% on Few-NERD; Appendix~\ref{app:cross_scale}).

\paragraph{Inverse scaling under adaptive thresholds.}
Increasing $N$ \textit{decreases} construction F1 in 40 of 58 transitions ($p{<}0.05$; Appendix~\ref{app:n_scaling}).
Methods using $\theta{=}2/N$ are most affected ($-3.09$\,pp at $N{=}8$, $-6.90$\,pp at $N{=}16$ vs.\ greedy on Few-NERD); majority vote ($\theta{=}\tfrac{1}{2}$) reduces the delta to $-0.87$/$-0.60$\,pp---severity reduction, not immunity (Table~\ref{tab:theta_sweep}).
Few-shot demonstrations improve QE (CoNLL SJ $\rho$ $.30{\to}.44$) but not construction (all n.s.; Appendix~\ref{app:fs_error}).

\subsection{Degeneracy Decomposition}\label{sec:theory}

\noindent\textbf{Degeneracy Decomposition.}\label{thm:degeneracy_bound}
For any aggregation method $A$: $\mathbb{E}[\Delta F_1(A)] \leq (1 - D) \cdot G_{\textup{nd}}$,
where $D$ is the degeneracy rate and $G_{\textup{nd}}$ the oracle gap on non-degenerate instances.
For signal-based methods with alignment $\alpha \in [0,1]$, this tightens to $(1{-}D) \cdot G_{\textup{nd}} \cdot \alpha$ (proof in Appendix~\ref{app:bound_proof}).

\noindent The decomposition is diagnostic rather than predictive: the three factors ($D$, $G_{\textup{nd}}$, $\alpha$) localize where aggregation fails rather than forecasting performance.
Across 16 configurations, all gains respect the bound but it captures only 3--12\% of actual gains (Figure~\ref{fig:bound}): $\alpha$---not $D$---is the bottleneck ($\alpha{=}0.000$--$0.161$), and the bound cannot be tightened by signal combination (\S\ref{sec:robustness_main}) or verifier-based construction (\S\ref{sec:verifier}).
Conclusions are stable across $\theta_{\text{crit}} \in [0.60, 0.80]$; signal alignment ($\alpha{\approx}0.068$) is the binding constraint, $8$--$33{\times}$ tighter than the degeneracy threshold.
The bound assumes entity-wise independence---an illustrative upper bound under simplifying assumptions; actual entity correlations (split-half $r{=}0.578$--$0.644$; bound ratio $0.13$--$0.63$) tighten the effective bound, as the 3--12\% recovery ratio indirectly reflects.

\noindent The following proposition provides motivating analysis under simplified assumptions; the empirical validation in Table~\ref{tab:alpha_crit} constitutes the primary evidence.

\begin{proposition}[Entity Selection Diagnostic]\label{prop:entity_selection_bound}
Let $\mathcal{M}^+ {=} \hat{y}_{\textup{MV}} \setminus \hat{y}_{\textup{gr}}$ be entities added by majority vote (precision $\pi_{\mathcal{M}^+}$) and $\delta$ the instance degeneracy rate.
\begin{enumerate}[label=\textup{(\alph*)},nosep,leftmargin=*]
\item On degenerate instances, $\hat{y}_{\textup{MV}} {=} \hat{y}_{\textup{gr}}$ and $\Delta F_1 {=} 0$.
\item $\mathbb{E}[\Delta F_1] = (1{-}\delta) \cdot \mathbb{E}[\Delta F_1 \mid \textup{non-deg.}]$.
\item $\Delta F_1 > 0$ only if $\pi_{\mathcal{M}^+} > \alpha_{\textup{crit}} \triangleq F_1^{\textup{gr}}/2$.
\end{enumerate}
\end{proposition}
\noindent\textit{Proof in Appendix~\ref{app:alpha_crit}.}
Table~\ref{tab:alpha_crit} validates: at $N{=}8$, MV does not improve F1 on any dataset ($\Delta$ from $-2.74$ to ${\approx}0$\,pp).
Defining $\alpha_{\textup{crit,eff}} {=} \alpha_{\textup{crit}} / (1{-}\delta)$, CoNLL ($\alpha_{\textup{crit,eff}}{=}1.148 {>} 1$) makes MV gain structurally impossible; the observed $\Delta{=}{-}0.04$\,pp confirms this.
Few-NERD and SciERC ($\alpha_{\textup{crit,eff}} {<} 1$) are negative ($-2.74$, $-0.74$\,pp): even where structural headroom exists, marginal-entity precision is insufficient.

\section{Related Work}\label{sec:related}

\paragraph{Test-time compute scaling.}
Self-consistency \cite{wang2023selfconsistency} and best-of-$N$ \cite{snell2025scaling, brown2024monkeys} drive large gains on reasoning and code \cite{chen2021codex}.
Reward-free selectors \cite{taubenfeld2025cisc, kang2025selfcertainty}, BoN scaling laws \cite{gao2023scaling}, and trained verifiers \cite{cobbe2021verifiers, lightman2024verify} target discrete-answer settings; NER requires different aggregation because degeneracy operates at the entity-set level.

\paragraph{SC failure modes.}
\citet{chen2023twofailures} identify SC failures where consistent outputs are wrong; free-form \cite{chen2024usc} and atomic \cite{thirukovalluru2024atomic} SC variants face limits where sub-output agreement does not yield selection gain (\S\ref{sec:selection_gap}).

\paragraph{Consistency for structured IE.}
\citet{yao2026consistency} show consistency correlates with accuracy for LLM-based NER/RE at the task level ($\rho$ up to 0.47); we extend this to instance-level analysis, diagnosing \textit{why} correlation does not transfer to selection gains. Key distinctions: (1)~granularity---task-level $\rho$ masks instance-level degeneracy; (2)~mechanism---we decompose failure into degeneracy and LP compression; (3)~actionability---pre-deployment diagnostics (measure degeneracy at $N{=}8$) that task-level correlation cannot provide.
\citet{xie2023zeroshot} propose entity voting for zero-shot NER and \citet{xie2024selfimproving} report positive SC where zero-shot diversity is high.
\paragraph{Uncertainty estimation and MBR.}
Our selection instantiates MBR decoding \cite{eikema2022sampling, denero2009fast}; despite success in translation \cite{freitag2022high} and code \cite{jinnai2024mbr}, all variants fail due to instance degeneracy (Appendix~\ref{app:mbr_connection}).
\citet{eikema2025structured} find MBR utilities degrade on structured outputs; our framework localizes this to degeneracy and LP compression (\S\ref{sec:selection_gap}).
LP is well-calibrated at population level (ECE $0.069$--$0.096$) but too compressed within instances for selection \cite{kadavath2022language, tian2023calibration}.
Recent work on token-level uncertainty transmission for sequential labeling and LLM-based NER self-verification further motivates entity-level rather than token-level quality signals.

\paragraph{Capability ceiling vs.\ diversity collapse.}\label{sec:yun_contrast}
\citet{yun2025price} identify \textit{diversity collapse} under constrained decoding for discrete answers (Table~\ref{tab:differentiation}).
Our degeneracy differs: they measure response-level diversity loss; we measure entity-set convergence eliminating selection signal, adding quantitative diagnostics ($D$, $\alpha$, LP compression) absent from their account.

\paragraph{Complementary approaches.}
Conformal prediction \cite{fisch2021conformal, angelopoulos2023conformal} provides coverage guarantees but requires exchangeability assumptions violated by degeneracy; token-level uncertainty operates below entity level; entity-level MBR with F1 utility is our entity construction (\S\ref{sec:mbr_theory}).

\paragraph{What is new.}
We provide the first instance-level diagnostic framework for NER, extending beyond task-level analysis \cite{yao2026consistency}, qualitative failure modes \cite{chen2023twofailures}, and discrete-answer diversity \cite{yun2025price}.

